== Serre's theorem on products of Bocksteins <sec:serre-bocksteins>
#idx("Serre", "'s theorem", target: [@sec:serre-bocksteins])#idx(
  "theorem",
  "Serre's",
  target: [@sec:serre-bocksteins],
)

#source(160)In this section we shall prove a theorem of Serre, which states that
if $G$ is a $p$-group which is not elementary abelian, then there exist non-zero
elements $x_1, dots, x_n in H^1 (G, FF_p)$, for some $n$, such that the product
of the Bocksteins is zero
$ beta(x_1) . beta(x_2) . dots . beta(x_n) = 0. $
Of course, if $G$ is elementary abelian, the Bocksteins of degree one elements
form a polynomial subring of $H^* (G, FF_p)$ (Section~3.5 of Volume~I) and so no
such relation exists.

Serre's original proof [@bib:Serre1965a] used Steenrod operations and Hilbert's
Nullstellensatz, and in particular gave no bound on $n$. Later, Kroll
[@bib:Kroll1986] gave a proof using Chern classes of complex representations,
which showed that it suffices to take each non-zero element of $H^1 (G, FF_p)$
once only in the above relation. Serre, not to be outdone, then showed in
[@bib:Serre1987] how to modify his original proof to show that it suffices to
take one non-zero element from each one dimensional subspace of $H^1 (G, FF_p)$.
Okuyama and Sasaki [@bib:Okuyama1990a] gave a proof which uses Evens' norm map
instead of the Steenrod operations, and the proof we shall give is a
modification by Evens of their proof, which gives a slight improvement on
Serre's bound. This proof proceeds by induction, starting with the case of a
group of order $p^3$.

#lemma[
  If $|G| = p^3$ and $|Phi(G)| = p$ then there are non-zero elements
  $x_1, dots, x_(p+1)$ of $H^1 (G, FF_p)$, such that
  $ beta(x_1) . beta(x_2) . dots . beta(x_(p+1)) = 0. $
] <lem:serre-order-p-cubed>

#proof[
  If $G$ has a cyclic maximal subgroup $H$ of order $p^2$, then there is a
  non-zero element $y in H^1 (H, FF_p)$ with $beta(y) = 0$. The element $y$ is
  the inflation of an element $macron(y) in H^1 (H slash Phi(H), FF_p)$, and so
  by Proposition~@prop:norm-properties[(vi)] and Proposition~@prop:norm-rank-two
  we have
  $
    0 & = norm_(H, G) (beta(y))
        = inf_(G slash Phi(G), G)
        norm_(H slash Phi(H), G slash Phi(G)) (beta(macron(y))) \
      & = inf_(G slash Phi(G), G)
        (beta(macron(y)_2)^p - beta(macron(y)_1)^(p-1) beta(macron(y)_2))
        = beta(y_2)^p - beta(y_1)^(p-1) beta(y_2) \
      & = product_(lambda=0)^(p-1) (beta(y_2 + lambda y_1)).
  $
  Here, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are suitably chosen generators for $H^1 (G, FF_p)$,
  which are inflations of generators $macron(y)_1$ and $macron(y)_2$ of
  $H^1 (G slash Phi(G), FF_p)$.

  If on the other hand $G$ has no elements of order $p^2$, then $G$ is generated
  by elements $g$ and $h$ of order $p$. Their commutator $z = [g, h]$ is central
  and of order $p$. Let $H$ be the subgroup generated by $h$ and $z$, so that
  $H$ is elementary abelian of order $p^2$. Then $H^* (H, FF_p)$ has generators
  $y_1$ and $y_2$ in degree one, and $x_1 = beta(y_1)$, $x_2 = beta(y_2)$ in
  degree two, with $y_1^2 = y_2^2 = 0$ if $p$ is odd, and $y_1^2 = x_1$,
  $y_2^2 = x_2$ if $p = 2$. We choose the notation so that conjugation by $g$
  #source(161)fixes $x_2$ and sends $x_1$ to $x_1 + x_2$. So by
  Proposition~@prop:norm-properties we have
  $
    #aligned-rows(
      ($norm_(H, G) (x_1)$, $= norm_(H, G) (x_1 + x_2)$),
      (
        $$,
        $= norm_(H, G) (x_1) + norm_(H, G) (x_2)
        - Tr_(H, G) (alpha)$,
      ),
    )
  $
  for some $alpha in H^(2p) (H, FF_p)$, and hence
  $ norm_(H, G) (x_2) = Tr_(H, G) (alpha). $

  Choose generators $macron(u)_1$, $macron(u)_2$ in degree one and
  $macron(v)_1 = beta(macron(u)_1)$, $macron(v)_2 = beta(macron(u)_2)$ in degree
  two for $H^* (G slash Phi(G), FF_p)$, and write $u_1$, $u_2$, $v_1$, $v_2$ for
  their inflations in $H^* (G, FF_p)$. Choose the notation so that
  $res_(G, H) (u_1) = 0$ and $res_(G, H) (u_2) = y_2$. Then we have
  $res_(G, H) (v_1) = 0$ and $res_(G, H) (v_2) = x_2$, and so by
  Proposition~@prop:norm-properties and Proposition~@prop:norm-rank-two we have
  $
    0 = Tr_(H, G) (res_(G, H) (v_1) . alpha) = v_1 . Tr_(H, G) (alpha)
    = v_1 . norm_(H, G) (x_2) \
    = inf_(G slash Phi(G), G) (macron(v)_1 .
      norm_(H slash Phi(G), G slash Phi(G))
      res_(G slash Phi(G), H slash Phi(G)) (macron(v)_2)) \
    = inf_(G slash Phi(G), G)
    (macron(v)_1 . (macron(v)_2^p - macron(v)_1^(p-1) macron(v)_2))
    = v_1 (v_2^p - v_1^(p-1) v_2)
    = beta(u_1) product_(lambda=0)^(p-1) beta(u_2 + lambda u_1).
  $
]

#lemma[
  If $E$ is an elementary abelian $p$-group and $E' lt.eq E$, then for any
  $x in H^1 (E', FF_p)$, $norm_(E', E) beta(x)$ is a product of $|E : E'|$
  Bocksteins of elements of $H^1 (E, FF_p)$.
] <lem:norm-bockstein-product>

#proof[
  By transitivity and multiplicativity of the norm
  (Proposition~@prop:norm-properties), we may assume $E'$ is maximal in $E$.
  Then $x$ is the inflation to $E'$ of some element
  $macron(x) in H^1 (E' slash N, FF_p)$, for some maximal subgroup $N$ of $E'$.
  So by Proposition~@prop:norm-properties[(vi)], it suffices to prove the lemma
  with $N = 1$, namely in the case $|E'| = p$, $|E| = p^2$. This case is dealt
  with in Proposition~@prop:norm-rank-two.
]

#remark[
  In fact it is easy to see from the above argument (or by symmetry) that
  $norm_(E', E) beta(x)$ is exactly the product of the Bocksteins of those
  elements whose restriction is $x$.
]

#theorem(title: [Serre, $dots$])[
  If $G$ is a $p$-group which is not elementary abelian, then there exist
  non-zero elements
  $ x_1, dots, x_n in H^1 (G, FF_p), $
  with $n <= ((p + 1) slash p^2)|G : Phi(G)|$, such that
  $ beta(x_1) . beta(x_2) . dots . beta(x_n) = 0. $
] <th:serre-bocksteins>

#proof[
  If $G$ is not elementary abelian, then $Phi(G) != 1$, and so $Phi(G)$ has a
  maximal subgroup $N$ of index $p$, normal in $G$. If there is such a relation
  in $G slash N$, then it inflates to such a relation in $G$, so without loss of
  generality $N = 1$, and $Phi(G) tilde.equiv ZZ slash p$.

  #source(162)If $G slash Phi(G)$ is cyclic, then $G$ is cyclic and
  $beta(x) = 0$ for any $x in H^1 (G, FF_p)$. The result follows since
  $1 <= ((p + 1) slash p^2) . p$. Otherwise, $G$ contains a subgroup $H$ of
  order $p^3$, containing $Phi(G)$, and such that $Phi(H) = Phi(G)$. By
  Lemma~@lem:serre-order-p-cubed, we can find elements
  $x_1, dots, x_(p+1) in H^1 (H, FF_p)$ such that
  $ beta(x_1) . beta(x_2) . dots . beta(x_(p+1)) = 0 $
  in $H^* (H, FF_p)$. Each $x_i$ is the inflation from $H slash Phi(G)$ of an
  element $macron(x)_i$, and so by Proposition~@prop:norm-properties we have
  $
    0 & = norm_(H, G) (beta(x_1) . dots . beta(x_(p+1))) \
      & = norm_(H, G) inf_(H slash Phi(G), H)
        (beta(macron(x)_1) . dots . beta(macron(x)_(p+1))) \
      & = inf_(G slash Phi(G), G) norm_(H slash Phi(G), G slash Phi(G))
        (beta(macron(x)_1) . dots . beta(macron(x)_(p+1))) \
      & = inf_(G slash Phi(G), G) (norm beta(macron(x)_1) . dots .
          norm beta(macron(x)_(p+1))).
  $
  Now by Lemma~@lem:norm-bockstein-product, each
  $norm_(H slash Phi(G), G slash Phi(G)) beta(macron(x)_i)$ is a product of
  Bocksteins of degree one elements of $H^* (G slash Phi(G), FF_p)$, and so the
  inflation is a product of Bocksteins of degree one elements of
  $H^* (G, FF_p)$. The number of Bocksteins involved in this expression is
  $
    (p + 1) . |G slash Phi(G) : H slash Phi(G)|
    = ((p + 1) slash p^2) . |G : Phi(G)|.
  $
]

#remark[
  The above proof easily shows that the elements $x_i$ produced are in distinct
  one dimensional subspaces of $H^1 (G, FF_p)$, although we have not taken the
  trouble to keep track of this in the argument.
]
